 "result_text": "I fixed the bad shifted timestamp in `pysubs2/substation.py`, in `ms_to_timestamp`. I didn't run the test suite or the original `--shift-back 1045ms` command on the example file.\n\nThe function split the time into hours, minutes, seconds and milliseconds first, then rounded the milliseconds to centiseconds. A value like 39.996 s therefore became `39.100` instead of carrying over to `40.00`. It now rounds the total milliseconds first and splits afterwards, so the carry works.\n\nA direct check of the writer gives `1:17:40.00` for 4659996 ms. Small values such as 1 ms still come out as `0:00:00.00`.\n\nI didn't look at the edge case where a time just under the maximum representable value rounds up past it.",
